{{-- The app shell. Every app/auth page (login, dashboard, settings, account,
     projects, issue) is a bare fragment rendered into the <main> below, which
     is the single container the stx SPA router swaps on navigation.

     Two rules follow from that, and both are load-bearing:

     1. A page fragment must NOT contain its own <main>. Two <main> elements
        give the router two candidate containers and the fragment swap breaks.
        Use a <div> for page-level content wrappers.

     2. Anything a page adds to the DOM at runtime must be mounted INSIDE
        <main> (see the logout/upgrade chrome in dashboard.stx). Nodes appended
        to document.body outlive the swap and paint stale, unstyled leftovers
        over the next page.

     3. In-app links use <StxLink to="...">, which renders a plain <a> carrying
        `data-stx-link`. The router matches [data-stx-link] FIRST, but a marked
        link still has to clear the opt-out set, so `data-no-router` on an
        StxLink is honoured (client.js:1334-1336; it used to be ignored). Any
        link that must not be intercepted is still simplest left a plain <a>
        (see below).

     Marketing pages (/, /features/*, /use-cases/*, /compare/*) are NOT
     fragments — each is a self-contained document with its own <head> and
     /marketing.css. Links from an app page to one of those cross a shell
     boundary the router cannot fragment-swap: the marketing stylesheet lives
     in a <head> the swap never brings along, so the page arrives unstyled.
     Those links therefore stay plain <a href="/">, and do a normal navigation.
     Same for the /api/auth/*/redirect OAuth links, which are server redirects:
     intercepting one fetches the redirect as a fragment instead of following
     it.

     Staying a plain <a> is the whole mechanism, because config/ui.ts now sets
     `router.interceptAllLinks: false` — an unmarked anchor is never claimed.
     That flag used to be `true`, which is why these links also carried
     `data-no-router`; converting the marketing site's nav and footer anchors to
     <StxLink> is what allowed it to be turned off, and the attributes have
     since been removed as dead weight. The four OAuth links keep theirs as a
     latch — see the comment in config/ui.ts. Unifying both shells onto one
     layout would remove the marketing-boundary exception entirely. --}}
<main>
@yield('content')
</main>

@yield('footer')

@stack('scripts')
